There’s a sharp analogy there, with one important correction: we didn’t really make wolves “respect power” so much as co-evolve a cooperation protocol with them.

Domestication selected wolves that could tolerate proximity, read human signals, cooperate, and eventually become extraordinarily good at navigating human social behavior. Humans supplied food, protection, and a niche; proto-dogs supplied detection, hunting assistance, defense, companionship, etc. Over generations, the relationship changed both sides.

Primates are harder in an interesting way because they already arrive with much of the machinery we’re trying to impose: coalitions, deception, reciprocity, status competition, alliance switching, memory for grievances, social learning, and political maneuvering. Another primate doesn’t merely encounter your hierarchy; it can model the hierarchy and start playing it.

So your observation generalizes into something useful:

Intelligence changes the control problem.

With increasing agency, durable coordination shifts roughly from:

force → conditioning → incentives → relationships → norms → legitimacy → shared purpose.

And there’s a paradox hiding inside it: the more capable the agent, the less reliable brute domination becomes. A sufficiently intelligent subordinate can pretend to comply, wait, coordinate, defect, reinterpret instructions, or change the game entirely.

That applies far beyond primates—to organizations, states, markets, militaries, and especially multi-agent AI design. The highest-order solution isn’t creating something that fears the handler. It’s creating a system in which cooperation remains the advantageous equilibrium even when nobody is holding the leash.